WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.660
Gary W. Goldstein: Before I moved to Los Angeles,
I was an attorney in San Francisco.

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:08.069
It was a very respected position.

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:09.760
It was a beautiful city.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.140
I had a great life.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.040
I had a nice income.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.840
I was set and I was deeply comfortable.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.770
And it scared the hell out of me.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.540
Had I stayed, I knew for certain (about me),
not true of all lawyers, not true of all people,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.540
what have you.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:32.050
I knew for me, I was going to wither from
the inside.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:37.670
But that was not the right life choice for
me on a soul level, on a spirit level, not

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:45.660
a role level or a goal level but on a soul
level it was…I had to leave.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:46.660
I had to flee.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:49.379
I had to run away, literally.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:50.379
And so I did.

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:57.210
I ran away and I wasn’t sure exactly how
it was going to work out but I thought, I’m

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:02.699
going to go to the far end of the state to
this crazy place called LA where I knew nobody

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:07.639
and I was going to pursue getting into the
film business because I so love storytelling.

00:01:07.639 --> 00:01:11.329
I’d grown up inside of books, I was a lover
of story.

00:01:11.329 --> 00:01:16.499
And most of my friends and colleagues and
certainly the other attorneys at the firm

00:01:16.499 --> 00:01:22.429
I worked at they all wagged their finger at
me and said “How can you do that?”

00:01:22.429 --> 00:01:24.210
“That’s so brave.”

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.210
“That’s so crazy.”

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.310
Or something?

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.009
We don’t understand.

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:31.729
And I said to them it’s not brave.

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:34.210
It’s the opposite.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.240
It’s…it’s…it’s survival.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:44.149
That for me to survive and thrive, I can’t
stay here.

00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:50.259
This is unsafe for me and it’s unsafe because
it’s so comfortable.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:53.529
I need to go to this foreign land called Los
Angeles.

00:01:53.529 --> 00:01:56.579
I need to go to a place where I am anonymous
and know nobody.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:02.520
I need to stretch my limbs and I need to see
if I can make it in this very highly-competitive

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.590
unknown world of filmmaking.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.649
Because that lights me up, from the inside
out.

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:12.060
I may fail.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:18.090
I may come crawling back on my knees begging
you to take me back Oh Friends of San Francisco.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.680
But on the other hand if I don’t do it,
I will never know and I’ll always regret

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.209
it.

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:31.540
That’s one version of many, many, many stories
I could tell about…and that’s just me.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:40.640
But I’ve seen it across most of the people
who’ve been a part of my life, that this

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.840
thing called the comfort zone is the most
dangerous zip code you can live in.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.299
Because it’s limiting by definition.

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:49.730
You get inured.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.099
You become habit-driven.

00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:56.739
You notice less.

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:03.819
You become busy with that which is familiar
and you build invisible walls around that

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:05.799
experience.

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:10.730
That becomes your longitude and your latitude
as opposed to self-defining us as an adventure,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:17.430
as an explorer, as someone who is going to
be a maverick and do the things that truly,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.420
truly express more deeply who you are and
that cannot be the same thing when you’re

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.200
17 as when you are 40 as when you’re 80.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.370
We are designed to evolve.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.769
We are designed to change.

00:03:29.769 --> 00:03:32.700
The world changes whether we like it or not.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.260
It’s good of us to understand that’s something
to be embraced and it’s like our friend.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.269
It’s not our enemy, it’s not poison to
our spirit.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:45.230
It’s the opposite, it’s an elixir.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:50.409
I’ve had multiple careers and I don’t
think I’m done yet.

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:55.569
And that’s why because I can’t predict
what my appetites will be 24 months from now

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:58.959
or five or ten years from now.

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:06.250
I know what I’m doing right now is exciting
to me but it always involves exploration,

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:12.150
it always involves and I say that not as necessarily
as a physical reality but it’s an emotional,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.150
psychological, educational reality.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:20.260
You know like “How can I be a better version
of me?”

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.130
That’s the job really for all of us.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:28.650
How do we become a better version of ourselves
and since we spend a majority of our waking

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:37.490
hours in the service of this thing we call
work and livelihood, boy what a great tapestry

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.550
to make sure that what you’re doing expands
you.

